  and "unit tested in Node" compatible — it's the load-bearing detail of the whole design.

  Public API, deliberately three functions:

  - [38;5;153mCurrentUser.set({ userId, username })[39m — records identity, stamps [38;5;153mloginAt[39m.
  - [38;5;153mCurrentUser.get()[39m — returns [38;5;153m{ userId, username, loginAt }[39m, or [38;5;153mnull[39m when nobody is logged in.
  - [38;5;153mCurrentUser.clear()[39m — forgets. No logout UI exists today; the function does, so adding one is not a store change.

  Internally the factory takes its storage as an argument defaulting to [38;5;153msessionStorage[39m, so tests inject a fake.

  [1mStored format[0m — one key, [38;5;153mcurrentUser[39m, holding [38;5;153m{ "v": 1, "userId": ..., "username": ..., "loginAt": ... }[39m. The [38;5;153mv[39m is
  what lets a later field addition be a non-event.

  [1mChanges to [38;5;153mapp.js[39m:[0m

  - [38;5;153mlogin(username, password)[39m gains a [38;5;153muserId[39m in its return: [38;5;153m{ success: true, userId: STUB_USER_ID, user: username }[39m,
    where [38;5;153mSTUB_USER_ID[39m is a single obviously-fake constant (not derived from the username — it must be impossible to
    mistake for real) with a comment tying it to the unused [38;5;153mAPI_ENDPOINT[39m. One constant for everyone is intentional: a
    plausible-looking per-user fake is the thing that quietly ships to production.
  - The submit handler, on success, calls [38;5;153mCurrentUser.set({ userId: result.userId, username })[39m.

  [1m[38;5;153mindex.html[0m — add [38;5;153m<script src="current-user.js"></script>[39m [3mbefore[0m [38;5;153mapp.js[39m. Ordering matters and the spec will say so.

  [1mData flow:[0m submit → validate → [38;5;153mlogin()[39m → on success [38;5;153mCurrentUser.set(...)[39m → any later code calls [38;5;153mCurrentUser.get()[39m.
  Future forms read; they never write.
